## Practical notes (10-03)

`Submission.Work.MotC2.InfPlaceSetup` is *in flux*: another agent is concurrently extending it with
`one_notMem_infIdealR`, `isMaximal_infIdealR`, `instIsPrimeInfIdealR`, `infPlace_mem_iff` and
`instIsLocalizationInfPlace`, i.e. with the lemmas listed above.  It is deliberately **not**
imported by this note (which must stay buildable), and a prover who needs the content of "Target 2"
should be prepared to prove it **self-containedly** in their own file: everything needed is
elementary and only uses

* `Work.MotC2.Defs` (`infCoordRing`, `infIdeal`, `infPlace`, `infU`, `infW`),
* `Work.MotC2.Relations` (`infW_sq_relation`),
* `Work.MotC2.exists_aeval_add_mul_infW`, `Work.MotC2.recip_poly_degree_le`,
  `Work.MotC2.algebraMap_smul_eq_aeval` (for "Target 1"),
* Mathlib's `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.CoordinateRing.smul_basis_eq_zero` and
  `IsLocalization.AtPrime.{isLocalRing, map_eq_maximalIdeal}` (for "Target 2").

If one defines the ideal locally (`I := Ideal.span {⟨infU E, ·⟩, ⟨infW E, ·⟩} : Ideal ↥(infCoordRing E)`
— this is `Work.MotC2.infIdealR E` from `InfPlaceSetup`), then "Target 1" plus Mathlib's
`Submodule.mem_span_pair` and the closure lemmas of `Subalgebra`/`Submodule` suffice; the whole
construction is the exact mirror of `affPlaceAt`/`affPlaceIdeal` in
`Submission/Work/MotC2/AffPlaceSetup.lean` (worth reading first — it is complete and is the
template for `infPlace`).
